### Cadastro de usuário por um business
#
# POST /clients/business  (o GET no mesmo path lista os usuários do business)
#
# Exige token de um cliente com role Business e escopo `client:create`.
#
# `owner_id` NÃO vai no corpo: é resolvido a partir do token, igual ao GET.
# Mandar owner_id aqui não tem efeito.
#
# `role` é enum protobuf sem UnmarshalJSON, então aceita apenas NÚMERO:
#   0 None | 1 User | 2 Admin | 3 SuperAdmin | 4 Business | 5 Manager
# Passar a string ("User") devolve 400 no decode.
#
# Senha:
#   generate_randomic_password = true  -> o serviço gera e `password` é ignorado
#   generate_randomic_password = false -> `password` é obrigatório, mínimo 8 chars
#
# send_email_access = true dispara o e-mail com as credenciais de acesso
# (template business_client_created no mailer).

### 1. Caso principal — senha gerada pelo sistema e e-mail de acesso enviado
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Maria Souza Andrade",
  "email": "roxajin239@primetor.com",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": true,
  "send_email_access": true
}

### 2. Senha definida manualmente, com e-mail de acesso
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Carlos Eduardo Lima",
  "email": "carlos.lima@example.com",
  "password": "senhaProvisoria123!",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": false,
  "send_email_access": true
}

### 3. Cadastro silencioso — sem disparar e-mail
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Joana Ribeiro Alves",
  "email": "joana.alves@example.com",
  "password": "senhaProvisoria123!",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": false,
  "send_email_access": false
}

### 4. Usuário com papel de Manager, vinculado a um gestor
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rafael Monteiro Dias",
  "email": "rafael.dias@example.com",
  "role": 5,
  "manager_id": "6a73c836d9d0d3892ccc3a8d",
  "generate_randomic_password": true,
  "send_email_access": true
}

### 5. Erro esperado 400 — nome com menos de 5 caracteres
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ana",
  "email": "ana@example.com",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": true,
  "send_email_access": false
}

### 6. Erro esperado 400 — e-mail inválido
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Pedro Henrique Castro",
  "email": "pedro-arroba-example",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": true,
  "send_email_access": false
}

### 7. Erro esperado 400 — senha curta sem geração automática
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Luciana Farias Nunes",
  "email": "luciana.nunes@example.com",
  "password": "123",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": false,
  "send_email_access": false
}

### 8. Erro esperado 401 — sem token
POST {{host}}/clients/business
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Sem Autenticacao Teste",
  "email": "sem.token@example.com",
  "role": 1,
  "generate_randomic_password": true,
  "send_email_access": false
}

### 9. Conferência — lista os usuários do business após o cadastro
GET {{host}}/clients/business
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}